WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
This week the direction flips.

2
00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:07.125
Until now your script called the model.
From here the model calls your code.

3
00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:11.600
That is one line of difference, and it
changes who is in charge.

4
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.350
Here is the Week 2 shape. You built the
message list.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.500
You chose when to call the model. It
produced text, and your script printed it.

6
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.975
Every decision was yours.

7
00:00:23.325 --> 00:00:28.300
Now the Week 3 shape. You add one line,
and you pass a list of tool schemas.

8
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.625
The reply can still be text. Or it can be
a request for one of your functions.

9
00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:36.821
The model did not change. The call you
send

10
00:00:36.821 --> 00:00:39.800
did not change much either. One argument
did.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.025
Four things moved when you added that
line.

12
00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:47.600
First, you passed a menu. The model now
knows what your tools are.

13
00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.830
Second, the reply can be a request instead
of

14
00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.775
an answer. Your code checks which one
arrived.

15
00:00:54.125 --> 00:00:59.000
Third, the tool name came from the model.
You never typed search tickets anywhere.

16
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.400
Fourth, the arguments came from the model
too. You never typed that query either.

17
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.079
Nothing about the model changed. What

18
00:01:07.079 --> 00:01:09.975
changed is where the next decision comes
from.

19
00:01:10.325 --> 00:01:14.750
Here is the part that trips people up. The
model did not run anything.

20
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.500
It produced text. That text named a
function and some arguments.

21
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.550
Your Python read the text, and your Python
made the call.

22
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.537
You still own every line that runs. You no

23
00:01:26.537 --> 00:01:29.425
longer own the choice of which line runs
next.

24
00:01:29.775 --> 00:01:33.900
That is the inversion, and it is what
makes an agent an agent.

25
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.675
If you keep one sentence from this week,
keep this one.

26
00:01:38.025 --> 00:01:40.850
The model asks. Your code answers.

27
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.850
Asking is free. Anyone whose text reaches
the model gets to ask.

28
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.650
Answering is where you decide, and
deciding is where security lives.

29
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.475
Now the one thing the model ever learns
about a tool.

30
00:01:54.825 --> 00:01:58.600
It never sees your Python. It never sees
your comments.

31
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.950
It reads the schema, and that is all it
gets.

32
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.179
So a schema is not documentation. It is an

33
00:02:05.179 --> 00:02:08.400
interface, and a promise your code has to
keep.

34
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.400
A schema fails in three ways, and you will
meet all three.

35
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:18.525
One. A vague description, so the tool runs
at the wrong moment, or never runs at all.

36
00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:22.124
Two. An argument with no description. The
model

37
00:02:22.124 --> 00:02:25.650
invents a value, and the value it invents
is wrong.

38
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.162
Three. A schema that does not match the
function.

39
00:02:29.162 --> 00:02:32.775
Your code then rejects exactly what the
schema promised.

40
00:02:33.125 --> 00:02:36.795
In Module 2 you write one of these
yourself. The autograder

41
00:02:36.795 --> 00:02:41.025
builds arguments from your schema and
calls your function with them.

42
00:02:41.375 --> 00:02:44.350
You do not have to take my word for any of
this.

43
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:50.450
Run python dash m agent dot replay. It
walks your last run one step at a time.

44
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.225
Next to the step where the model asked for
a tool, it prints one line.

45
00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:58.000
Nothing in your program chose this.

46
00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.825
This is the Week 1 picture with one new
box in it.

47
00:03:02.175 --> 00:03:03.625
You ask.

48
00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:06.950
The model picks a tool and its arguments.

49
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.624
Then before tool call decides. It runs
after the

50
00:03:10.624 --> 00:03:14.225
arguments are parsed, one line before the
tool runs.

51
00:03:14.575 --> 00:03:17.900
That gap is the last moment anything can
be stopped.

52
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:23.625
Then the tool runs. A record is read, or
an email is queued, and there is no undo.

53
00:03:23.975 --> 00:03:27.775
The result becomes another message, and
the loop runs again.

54
00:03:28.125 --> 00:03:32.125
Right now that function says yes to
everything, every time.

55
00:03:32.475 --> 00:03:34.575
You do not change it this week.

56
00:03:34.925 --> 00:03:38.600
You come back to it in Module 4, four
weeks from now.

57
00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:42.025
Learn where it sits, and remember one
thing about

58
00:03:42.025 --> 00:03:45.225
it. A blocked call still gets a line in
the ledger.

59
00:03:45.575 --> 00:03:48.600
That line is your proof the guard did
something.

60
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.454
Your job this week is smaller than it

61
00:03:51.454 --> 00:03:54.500
sounds. Get one real tool call into your
log.

62
00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:59.875
That is the checkpoint, and it is pass or
fail. Working code is not the bar.
